{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '460,860p' app/page.tsx\"","aggregated_output":"  useEffect(() => {\n    let timer: number | undefined;\n    try {\n      const saved = window.localStorage.getItem(\"assistant-study-progress\");\n      const parsed = saved ? JSON.parse(saved) : [];\n      if (Array.isArray(parsed)) {\n        timer = window.setTimeout(\n          () => setCompleted(parsed.filter((item) => typeof item === \"string\")),\n          0,\n        );\n      }\n    } catch {\n      // Progress is a convenience. The plan still works without browser storage.\n    }\n    return () => {\n      if (timer !== undefined) window.clearTimeout(timer);\n    };\n  }, []);\n\n  const completeCount = completed.length;\n  const percent = Math.round((completeCount / stages.length) * 100);\n  const progressText = useMemo(\n    () => `${completeCount} of ${stages.length} stages complete`,\n    [completeCount],\n  );\n\n  function toggleStage(id: string) {\n    const next = completed.includes(id)\n      ? completed.filter((item) => item !== id)\n      : [...completed, id];\n    setCompleted(next);\n    try {\n      window.localStorage.setItem(\n        \"assistant-study-progress\",\n        JSON.stringify(next),\n      );\n    } catch {\n      // Ignore storage failures.\n    }\n  }\n\n  function resetProgress() {\n    setCompleted([]);\n    try {\n      window.localStorage.removeItem(\"assistant-study-progress\");\n    } catch {\n      // Ignore storage failures.\n    }\n  }\n\n  return (\n    <>\n      <a className=\"skip-link\" href=\"#main-content\">\n        Skip to the plan\n      </a>\n\n      <header className=\"site-header\">\n        <a className=\"brand\" href=\"#top\" aria-label=\"Coding assistant study home\">\n          <span className=\"brand-mark\">LAB / 01</span>\n          <span>Coding assistant study</span>\n        </a>\n        <div className=\"header-actions\">\n          <div className=\"mini-progress\" aria-label={progressText}>\n            <span>{progressText}</span>\n            <span className=\"mini-track\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\n              <span style={{ width: `${percent}%` }} />\n            </span>\n          </div>\n          <button className=\"text-button print-button\" onClick={() => window.print()}>\n            Print / save PDF\n          </button>\n        </div>\n      </header>\n\n      <div className=\"page-shell\" id=\"top\">\n        <aside className=\"side-nav\" aria-label=\"Plan sections\">\n          <p className=\"eyebrow\">On this page</p>\n          <nav>\n            {navItems.map(([id, label], index) => (\n              <a href={`#${id}`} key={id}>\n                <span>{String(index + 1).padStart(2, \"0\")}</span>\n                {label}\n              </a>\n            ))}\n          </nav>\n          <div className=\"side-note\">\n            <p>Evidence status</p>\n            <strong>Through T6</strong>\n            <span>plus one distributed workflow</span>\n          </div>\n        </aside>\n\n        <main id=\"main-content\">\n          <section className=\"hero\" aria-labelledby=\"hero-title\">\n            <div className=\"hero-copy\">\n              <p className=\"kicker\">A live, reproducible benchmark</p>\n              <h1 id=\"hero-title\">\n                Find the right <em>assistant</em>, the right <em>model</em>, and the\n                right way to combine them.\n              </h1>\n              <p className=\"hero-lede\">\n                A repeatable study of Codex, Claude Code, and OpenCode—with\n                frontier models in the lead and hosted open models as practical\n                workers. The plan and the evidence now live together.\n              </p>\n              <div className=\"hero-actions\">\n                <a className=\"primary-button\" href=\"#results\">\n                  See the findings\n                </a>\n                <a className=\"secondary-button\" href=\"#design\">\n                  See the study design\n                </a>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n            <div className=\"hero-brief\" aria-label=\"Key recommendation\">\n              <p className=\"eyebrow\">Current recommendation</p>\n              <p className=\"brief-big\">Use a small team with clear roles.</p>\n              <p>\n                Codex leads and integrates. Claude challenges plans and reviews.\n                OpenCode routes bounded work to the best hosted open model.\n              </p>\n              <dl>\n                <div>\n                  <dt>Default lead</dt>\n                  <dd>Codex + GPT-5.6 Sol</dd>\n                </div>\n                <div>\n                  <dt>Reviewer</dt>\n                  <dd>Claude Code + Opus 5</dd>\n                </div>\n                <div>\n                  <dt>Open worker</dt>\n                  <dd>OpenCode + selected Kimi route</dd>\n                </div>\n              </dl>\n            </div>\n          </section>\n\n          <section className=\"section-block results-section\" id=\"results\" aria-labelledby=\"results-title\">\n            <div className=\"section-heading split-heading\">\n              <div>\n                <p className=\"section-number\">Results / Current</p>\n                <h2 id=\"results-title\">What the recorded runs actually showed.</h2>\n              </div>\n              <p>\n                These are one-run pilot findings, not universal rankings. Every\n                failure, timeout, permission denial, and grader correction remains\n                part of the evidence.\n              </p>\n            </div>\n\n            <div className=\"verdict-grid\">\n              <article className=\"verdict-card verdict-primary\">\n                <span>Recommended lead</span>\n                <h3>Codex</h3>\n                <p>\n                  Best default for controlled implementation and integration. It\n                  won the T6 frontier comparison on speed and tool discipline.\n                </p>\n              </article>\n              <article className=\"verdict-card\">\n                <span>Independent reviewer</span>\n                <h3>Claude Code</h3>\n                <p>\n                  Strong planning challenge and careful final evidence. Explicit\n                  tool authorization matters in narrow automated sessions.\n                </p>\n              </article>\n              <article className=\"verdict-card verdict-open\">\n                <span>Open-model route</span>\n                <h3>OpenCode</h3>\n                <p>\n                  A strong secondary tool, not a novelty. Kimi completed the T5\n                  implementation with zero Codex repair.\n                </p>\n              </article>\n            </div>\n\n            <div className=\"result-metrics\" aria-label=\"Headline benchmark findings\">\n              <div>\n                <strong>3 × 100</strong>\n                <span>Sol, Opus, and Kimi K3 on corrected T4</span>\n              </div>\n              <div>\n                <strong>3.57×</strong>\n                <span>Distributed T5 latency versus solo Codex</span>\n              </div>\n              <div>\n                <strong>0 edits</strong>\n                <span>Frontier repairs needed for Kimi&apos;s T5 patch</span>\n              </div>\n              <div>\n                <strong>$1.3765</strong>\n                <span>Metered OpenRouter spend; subscriptions separate</span>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n\n            <div className=\"table-wrap results-table-wrap\" tabIndex={0} aria-label=\"Selected benchmark results\">\n              <table>\n                <thead>\n                  <tr>\n                    <th>Task</th>\n                    <th>Assistant + model</th>\n                    <th>Score</th>\n                    <th>Time</th>\n                    <th>What mattered</th>\n                  </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                  {resultRows.map((row) => (\n                    <tr key={`${row.task}-${row.route}`}>\n                      <td>{row.task}</td>\n                      <td><strong>{row.route}</strong></td>\n                      <td><span className=\"result-score\">{row.score}</span></td>\n                      <td>{row.time}</td>\n                      <td>{row.note}</td>\n                    </tr>\n                  ))}\n                </tbody>\n              </table>\n            </div>\n\n            <div className=\"evidence-summary-grid\">\n              <article>\n                <span>Distributed workflow</span>\n                <h3>Useful for review-heavy work, not every task.</h3>\n                <p>\n                  Distributed T5 and solo Codex both scored 100. The multi-tool\n                  route took 536.704 seconds versus 150.206 seconds, but created a\n                  clearer plan challenge, ownership trail, and independent review.\n                </p>\n              </article>\n              <article>\n                <span>Portable extensions</span>\n                <h3>One skill and MCP server worked across all three.</h3>\n                <p>\n                  Codex and OpenCode passed immediately. Claude first exposed a\n                  permission setup trap, then passed a frozen recovery after only\n                  the two read-only tools were preauthorized.\n                </p>\n              </article>\n              <article>\n                <span>Small-model lesson</span>\n                <h3>Model and harness both matter.</h3>\n                <p>\n                  Kimi K3 earned a full T4 result in OpenCode, while Qwen3.8-27B\n                  timed out. With Kimi K2.7 held fixed on T6, all three harnesses\n                  passed and OpenCode was fastest.\n                </p>\n              </article>\n            </div>\n\n            <div className=\"callout plain result-caveat\">\n              <strong>Decision now:</strong> Codex is the primary lead, Claude Code\n              is the primary independent reviewer and alternate lead, and OpenCode\n              is a strong secondary tool for hosted open-model work. More repeats\n              are needed before claiming a general winner.\n            </div>\n          </section>\n\n          <section className=\"section-block\" id=\"scope\">\n            <div className=\"section-heading\">\n              <p className=\"section-number\">01 / Scope</p>\n              <h2>Four programs, with three in the final ranking.</h2>\n              <p>\n                Here, “assistant” or “harness” means the program around the model:\n                its tools, planning, permissions, memory, plugins, and user\n                experience. Pi is a control instrument, not a fourth primary winner.\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <div className=\"assistant-grid\">\n              {assistantCards.map((assistant) => (\n                <article className=\"assistant-card\" key={assistant.name}>\n                  <div className=\"assistant-top\">\n                    <span className=\"assistant-mark\">{assistant.mark}</span>\n                    <span className=\"role-pill\">{assistant.role}</span>\n                  </div>\n                  <h3>{assistant.name}</h3>\n                  <p>{assistant.summary}</p>\n                  <div className=\"test-note\">\n                    <span>Inspect</span>\n                    <p>{assistant.test}</p>\n                  </div>\n                </article>\n              ))}\n            </div>\n            <div className=\"callout plain\">\n              <strong>Boundary:</strong> compare Codex, Claude Code, and OpenCode\n              as the products you may keep using. Use Pi to make the model test\n              fair and to explore custom orchestration.\n            </div>\n          </section>\n\n          <section className=\"section-block\" id=\"models\">\n            <div className=\"section-heading split-heading\">\n              <div>\n                <p className=\"section-number\">02 / Models</p>\n                <h2>A small, current model shortlist.</h2>\n              </div>\n              <p>\n                Exact catalogs change quickly. Record the full model ID visible on\n                the day of each run. “Open weight” is used for downloadable models\n                because their licenses and definitions of open source differ.\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <div className=\"table-wrap model-table-wrap\" tabIndex={0}>\n              <table>\n                <thead>\n                  <tr>\n                    <th>Model</th>\n                    <th>Class</th>\n                    <th>Access</th>\n                    <th>Use in this study</th>\n                    <th>Priority</th>\n                  </tr>\n                </thead>\n                <tbody>\n                  {modelRows.map((row) => (\n                    <tr key={row.model}>\n                      <td>\n                        <strong>{row.model}</strong>\n                        <span>{row.maker}</span>\n                      </td>\n                      <td>{row.lane}</td>\n                      <td>{row.access}</td>\n                      <td>{row.use}</td>\n                      <td>\n                        <span\n                          className={`priority priority-${row.priority.toLowerCase()}`}\n                        >\n                          {row.priority}\n                        </span>\n                      </td>\n                    </tr>\n                  ))}\n                </tbody>\n              </table>\n            </div>\n            <div className=\"model-rule-grid\">\n              <div>\n                <span>Hardware rule</span>\n                <p>\n                  With 16 GB of unified memory, keep large open-weight models in\n                  Ollama Cloud. Use Qwen3 8B only as a labeled small local control,\n                  not as a substitute inside the shared-model lane.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n              <div>\n                <span>Budget rule</span>\n                <p>\n                  Use subscription access first. Use Ollama Cloud for Kimi and\n                  DeepSeek. Treat Fable, Luna, Haiku, and paid APIs as optional until\n                  the pilot proves they add a useful comparison.\n                </p>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </section>\n\n          <section className=\"section-block\" id=\"design\">\n            <div className=\"section-heading\">\n              <p className=\"section-number\">03 / Study design</p>\n              <h2>Three lanes prevent the wrong conclusion.</h2>\n              <p>\n                A great result may come from the model, the surrounding program, or\n                both. These lanes separate those effects before you judge the full\n                product.\n              </p>\n            </div>\n            <div className=\"lane-list\">\n              {benchmarkLanes.map((lane) => (\n                <article className=\"lane-card\" key={lane.number}>\n                  <span className=\"lane-number\">{lane.number}</span>\n                  <div className=\"lane-content\">\n                    <div className=\"lane-title-row\">\n                      <h3>{lane.title}</h3>\n                      <span>{lane.lock}</span>\n                    </div>\n                    <p>{lane.body}</p>\n                    <div className=\"lane-meta\">\n                      <p>\n                        <strong>Target:</strong> {lane.runs}\n                      </p>\n                      <p>\n                        <strong>Answers:</strong> {lane.answers}\n                      </p>\n                    </div>\n                  </div>\n                </article>\n              ))}\n            </div>\n            <div className=\"method-strip\">\n              <div>\n                <span>Same start</span>\n                <p>Clean commit and new session</p>\n              </div>\n              <div>\n                <span>Same prompt</span>\n                <p>Versioned and hashed</p>\n              </div>\n              <div>\n                <span>Two runs</span>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"agent_message","text":"## Product interpretation\n\nThe `/trade-capture` workspace supports a local lifecycle: enter or edit a draft → validate and calculate economics → save or book → filter/select from today’s blotter → review economics, exceptions, allocations, and audit history → cancel booked trades without deleting history.\n\nSimplifying assumptions:\n\n- USD only; no persistence beyond page reload and no external data.\n- The six product values are fixed identifiers: `agency-rmbs`, `non-agency-rmbs`, `cmbs`, `abs`, `clo`, `tba-mbs`.\n- Dates are ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`; timestamps are ISO strings. No holiday-calendar or settlement-convention engine.\n- Numeric form blanks use `null`; calculations retain JavaScript number precision and formatting occurs only in components.\n- A missing optional client trade ID produces the non-blocking operational warning needed by seed data; no regulatory-compliance warning is implied.\n- Allocation tolerance is `0.01`; cancelled trades remain visible but are excluded from totals.\n\nThis follows the frozen calculations, rules, lifecycle, and synthetic-data boundary in `benchmark/projects/T7-securitized-trade-capture/PRODUCT.md`.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe existing project is a strict TypeScript React 19/Next 16 application built through vinext; `app/page.tsx` is the client-rendered benchmark dashboard, while `app/layout.tsx` supplies shared fonts and metadata. Scripts in `package.json` already provide typecheck, ESLint, production build, Node tests, and the aggregate verification gate. `tests/rendered-html.test.mjs` tests the built worker through `worker.fetch`.\n\nAdd a separate route:\n\n```text\napp/trade-capture/page.tsx                 server route shell + metadata\n└── TradeCaptureApp                       sole client state owner\n    ├── DeskInsights\n    ├── TradeTicket\n    ├── validation summary\n    ├── TradeBlotter\n    └── TradeReview\n```\n\n`page.tsx` remains a server component and imports the route stylesheet and client application. `TradeCaptureApp` owns a reducer containing trades, ticket draft, selected ID, filters, search, and sort. No server actions, storage, API routes, database, or network calls.\n\nPure domain code lives under `app/trade-capture/domain/`, with no React imports. Route styles use `tc-`-prefixed selectors beneath `.tc-shell`, protecting the existing dashboard styles in `app/globals.css`.\n\n## Stable contracts\n\nFreeze these exports before Sonnet, Qwen, and Kimi begin:\n\n```ts\nexport type ProductType =\n  | \"agency-rmbs\" | \"non-agency-rmbs\" | \"cmbs\"\n  | \"abs\" | \"clo\" | \"tba-mbs\";\nexport type TradeStatus = \"draft\" | \"validated\" | \"booked\" | \"cancelled\";\nexport type Side = \"buy\" | \"sell\";\nexport type TradeModifier =\n  | \"regular\" | \"specified-pool\" | \"stipulation\"\n  | \"dollar-roll\" | \"weighted-average-price\";\nexport type ExceptionSeverity = \"error\" | \"warning\";\n\nexport interface AllocationInput {\n  id: string; label: string; currentFace: number | null;\n}\nexport interface Allocation {\n  id: string; label: string; currentFace: number;\n}\nexport interface TradeException {\n  code: string;\n  severity: ExceptionSeverity;\n  field: keyof TradeDraft | \"allocations\";\n  message: string;\n}\nexport interface AuditEvent {\n  id: string;\n  at: string;\n  action: \"created\" | \"draft-saved\" | \"validated\" | \"booked\" | \"cancelled\";\n  detail: string;\n}\nexport interface TradeEconomics {\n  currentFace: number;\n  grossPrincipal: number;\n  signedExposure: number;\n}\nexport interface TradeDraft {\n  internalTradeId: string | null;\n  clientTradeId: string;\n  executionTimestamp: string;\n  tradeDate: string;\n  settlementDate: string;\n  side: Side;\n  productType: ProductType;\n  securityId: string;\n  issuerDeal: string;\n  trancheClass: string;\n  counterparty: string;\n  book: string;\n  trader: string;\n  currency: \"USD\";\n  originalFace: number | null;\n  factor: number | null;\n  price: number | null;\n  coupon: number | null;\n  spreadBps: number | null;\n  accruedInterest: number | null;\n  agency: string;\n  poolNumber: string;\n  weightedAverageLife: number | null;\n  collateralType: string;\n  rating: string;\n  modifier: TradeModifier;\n  allocations: AllocationInput[];\n  note: string;\n}\nexport type Trade = Omit<TradeDraft, \"internalTradeId\" | \"allocations\"> & {\n  internalTradeId: string;\n  status: TradeStatus;\n  allocations: Allocation[];\n  economics: TradeEconomics;\n  exceptions: TradeException[];\n  auditEvents: AuditEvent[];\n  synthetic: true;\n};\nexport interface DeskSummary {\n  activeTradeCount: number;\n  bookedTradeCount: number;\n  exceptionCount: number;\n  buyExposure: number;\n  sellExposure: number;\n  netExposure: number;\n  grossPrincipal: number;\n}\n```\n\nDomain exports:\n\n```ts\ncalculateEconomics(\n  originalFace: number, factor: number, price: number, side: Side\n): TradeEconomics;\nvalidateTrade(draft: TradeDraft): TradeException[];\nmaterializeTrade(\n  draft: TradeDraft,\n  context: { id: string; status: TradeStatus; at: string }\n): Trade;\nsummarizeDesk(trades: readonly Trade[]): DeskSummary;\nEMPTY_TRADE_DRAFT: Readonly<TradeDraft>;\nSEED_TRADES: readonly Trade[];\n```\n\nComponent props:\n\n```ts\nTradeTicketProps {\n  draft: TradeDraft;\n  exceptions: readonly TradeException[];\n  onChange(next: TradeDraft): void;\n  onSaveDraft(): void;\n  onValidate(): void;\n  onBook(): void;\n  onReset(): void;\n}\nDeskInsightsProps { summary: DeskSummary; }\nTradeBlotterProps {\n  trades: readonly Trade[];\n  selectedTradeId: string | null;\n  product: ProductType | \"all\";\n  status: TradeStatus | \"all\";\n  search: string;\n  sort: \"execution-desc\" | \"current-face-desc\" | \"gross-principal-desc\";\n  onProductChange(value: ProductType | \"all\"): void;\n  onStatusChange(value: TradeStatus | \"all\"): void;\n  onSearchChange(value: string): void;\n  onSortChange(value: TradeBlotterProps[\"sort\"]): void;\n  onSelect(id: string): void;\n}\nTradeReviewProps {\n  trade: Trade | null;\n  onEditDraft(id: string): void;\n  onCancel(id: string): void;\n}\n```\n\n## File ownership\n\n- Terra:\n  `app/trade-capture/domain/types.ts`,\n  `calculations.ts`, `validation.ts`, `seed.ts`, `index.ts`,\n  `tests/trade-domain.test.ts`.\n- Sonnet:\n  `app/trade-capture/components/trade-ticket.tsx`.\n- Qwen:\n  `app/trade-capture/components/desk-insights.tsx`.\n- Kimi:\n  `app/trade-capture/trade-capture-app.tsx`,\n  `components/trade-blotter.tsx`,\n  `components/trade-review.tsx`,\n  `trade-capture.css`.\n- Sol integrator:\n  `app/trade-capture/page.tsx`, `package.json`,\n  `tests/rendered-html.test.mjs`.\n\nNo worker may modify another worker’s files, existing dashboard files, lockfiles, benchmark evidence, configuration, or integrator-owned files.\n\n## Worker tasks\n\n- Terra implements all rules, calculations, immutable seed fixtures, six-product coverage, and boundary/status tests. It may run `node --test tests/trade-domain.test.ts`, `npm run typecheck`, and `npm run lint`. It must not add UI or dependencies.\n- Sonnet builds a fully labelled ticket using semantic `form`, `fieldset`, inputs, selects, inline error associations, and real buttons. Booking is disabled when errors exist. It may run typecheck and lint after receiving Terra’s frozen types. It must not calculate, validate, own state, or style other panels.\n- Qwen renders only the supplied `DeskSummary`, with clear signed/currency formatting and meaningful headings. It may run typecheck and lint. It must not derive totals, mutate data, or expand into dashboard/state work.\n- Kimi implements reducer-driven lifecycle, filtering, search, sorting, row selection, cancellation history, review, and the responsive visual system. It may run typecheck, lint, build, and existing tests. It must consume domain functions rather than duplicate their rules and must not alter the ticket or insights implementations.\n\n## Integration sequence\n\nSol first applies Terra’s patch, runs its tests, freezes the domain export surface, and gives that exact revision to the other three workers. Sol then combines Sonnet and Qwen, followed by Kimi, and finally adds the route, test-script wiring, and rendered assertions.\n\nFor an interface mismatch, Sol records which declared contract and worker output disagree. Prefer adapting the consumer in its owning patch. If acceptance requires editing worker-owned code, Sol makes the smallest repair, reruns that worker’s checks, and reports the changed file and original failure. It must not silently normalize incompatible patches or report the worker as independently passing.\n\n## Verification\n\nIsolated branches:\n\n- Terra: calculation precision, signed exposure, all validation bounds, CUSIP shape, dates, agency/modifier rules, allocation tolerance, warnings, cancelled-total exclusion, and seed coverage.\n- Other workers: `npm run typecheck` and targeted lint after rebasing onto Terra; Kimi also runs `npm run build`.\n\nFinal integrated gate:\n\n1. `node --test tests/trade-domain.test.ts`\n2. `npm run typecheck`\n3. `npm run lint`\n4. `npm run build`\n5. `node --test tests/rendered-html.test.mjs`\n6. `npm run benchmark:validate`\n7. `npm run verify`\n\nExtend `tests/rendered-html.test.mjs` to request both `/` and `/trade-capture`; preserve current dashboard assertions and assert the synthetic notice, totals, ticket, validation region, blotter, review heading, and initial seeded records.\n\nManual checks cover every workflow action, keyboard-only use, focus visibility, error announcement/association, cancellation history, sorting/filtering/search, reload reset, console errors, desktop layout, and 390-pixel width without page overflow. No build or test command was run during this read-only planning task because builds create artifacts.\n\n## Risks and review focus\n\n1. Floating-point and blank-number handling could violate full-precision calculations or the one-cent allocation tolerance.\n2. Product/modifier validation and lifecycle transitions could diverge between domain code and reducer logic.\n3. Seed or generated records could accidentally resemble real client, trader, account, or regulatory data.\n4. Cross-worker contract or CSS drift could yield a type-correct but visually inaccessible integration.\n5. Client hydration, timestamp generation, responsive tables, or route-wide styles could break rendered HTML, mobile usability, or the existing `/` benchmark dashboard."}}
